**Read:** [Nano-to-Macro Transport Processes — MIT 2.57](../../../physics/thermodynamics-and-heat-transfer/sources/Nano-to-Macro-Transport-MIT-2-57.lingua#r_1YF7TD689ZRGQ51D4B3NGVHZQG), coupled charge/heat transport; [Functional material response — Cambridge DoITPoMS](../sources/Functional-Response-DoITPoMS.lingua#r_6WMQKCE9PFYYETKPTR50DH6RTM), piezoelectric and pyroelectric applications; [Electron interactions, magnetism and superconductivity — Daniel C. Ralph](../sources/Interacting-Electrons-Magnetism-Superconductivity-Ralph.lingua#r_3B28YDT2Z6KYBRZ8DHM1069TW1), material-device examples. Follow the shared thermodynamic coupled-flux Record.

A useful stack may need to conduct heat, block electrical leakage, transmit light, adhere to another material and survive strain simultaneously. Improvements in one response can worsen another. A filler that increases conductivity can change stiffness, interface losses, dielectric strength or processability.

Coupled coefficients must use compatible constraints and conventions. Positivity of dissipation restricts admissible near-equilibrium transport matrices; reversible couplings need their own energy account. Mechanical restraint, external fields and temperature gradients can change the measured effective response.

**Work:** Construct one stack-level account for an electronic package, a sensor or an energy-conversion element. Identify bulk and interface parameters, boundary conditions and uncertainties. Compare two feasible material states and explain a tradeoff through mechanisms rather than a single weighted catalogue score.

**Return:** [Materials engineering — structure, processing and useful behavior](../Materials-engineering.lingua#r_05BF5JM8141RKF1VT5998YFZQ9).
